WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.000
We introduce CAST, which improves robot skills while keeping the original
policy as a reference.

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:11.000
This matters because the critic can misjudge which actions are better.

00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:15.000
As orange shows, even small updates can drift away.

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:18.000
CAST pulls back, as green shows.

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:23.000
So we keep the original policy fixed and learn a correction.

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:26.000
Each action keeps its own reference.

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:28.000
Here’s one learning step.

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:31.000
We first sample paired actions.

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:37.000
Next, the critic proposes an improvement. We normalize, cap, and scale its
gradient.

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:40.000
We also pull toward the base,

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:44.000
with a stronger pull beyond the reference radius.

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:50.000
We combine these arrows and cap the target step around the current action.

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:54.000
We then fit the correction to these fixed targets,

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:56.000
and repeat the process.

00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:01.000
The policy now learns to match the fixed green targets.

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:03.000
We refresh them and repeat,

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:06.000
while the soft pull leaves room to improve.

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:12.000
Now let’s look at the experiments. In LIBERO, both policies start from the
same state.

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:17.000
CAST places the bottle in the drawer; the base fails.

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:21.000
To see how learning progresses, robomimic compares three CAST checkpoints.

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:27.000
The early policy struggles, while later checkpoints complete the nut
insertion.

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:38.000
Beyond manipulation, DMC tests locomotion with dense rewards. As CAST
learns, the walker progresses from unsteady movement toward running.

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:41.000
The measured results show the same pattern.

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:50.000
In robomimic square, CAST reaches ninety percent success using thirty-eight
percent fewer environment steps than DICE-RL.

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:55.000
Across five LIBERO tasks, no controls or clipping alone gives zero success,

00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:59.000
while CAST improves every task over the base.

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:04.000
We next move from simulation to OpenArm, using chest and wrist cameras.

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:10.000
CAST learns offline from recorded successes and failures before deployment.

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:15.000
We now show the CAST policies completing placement and stacking.

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:20.000
For placement, the robot reaches down and grasps Jigglypuff.

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:23.000
It carries the toy over the case,

00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:26.000
then releases it fully inside.

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:30.000
For stacking, it grasps the red cube,

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:34.000
then aligns it above the purple cube.

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:37.000
The gripper opens, completing the stack.

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:44.000
Looking at the overall results, CAST raises placement success from 55 to 95
percent.

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:51.000
Stacking improves from 35 to 70 percent, again exceeding both refinement
baselines.

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:55.000
In short, we guide improvement, limit each target step,

00:02:55.000 --> 00:02:59.000
and keep the original skill as a reference.

